    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a high ceiling, anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (82.13), who draws a favorable matchup against McGreevy. **Corbin Carroll** is our premier play, benefiting from an elite park factor and a dynamic platoon advantage that pushes him well into the 80s. Alongside him, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) are clear starts; **Mickey Moniak** is feasting on the projections thanks to a perfect storm of ballpark benefits and a favorable lineup slot, while **Seiya Suzuki** leverages a significant SP skill gap against Ober and favorable wind conditions to drive his efficiency well above the baseline.\n\nThe lineup optimization remains aggressive, though **Austin Riley** (66.75) carries a 30% rain risk warning that could force a late-game pivot. While **Josh Naylor** (58.11) was the most difficult exclusion from the starting nine, he ultimately hit the bench because his overall efficiency against Roupp failed to surpass the high-floor production of our middle-of-the-pack starters. **Jose Caballero** (56.27) and **Kyle Karros** (59.51) remain in the lineup over bench options due to their specific skill sets\u2014**Jose Caballero** continues to be a staple for us due to his stolen base upside against a pitcher with a notoriously slow delivery, a factor that heavily influences our selection strategy given the scarcity of speed in this league.\n\nWe are keeping a close eye on the bottom of the order, specifically **Dalton Rushing** (48.93) and **Edwin Arroyo** (49.90). Both players are hovering near our \"Marginal\" threshold. While their raw projections were dampened by unfavorable lineup positioning, the park factors remain robust enough to justify the start in hopes of preserving our game caps without sacrificing our core categories. If the weather reports for **Austin Riley** worsen, we will look to rotate in **Josh Naylor** or **Isaac Paredes** to maintain our competitive edge in HR and RBI production.",
